<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Assyrian conquest of Egypt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">671 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> · Also known as Esarhaddon's conquest of Egypt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Assyrian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was the invasion of Egypt by the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esarhaddon">Esarhaddon</a>, who captured Memphis in 671 BCE and drove out the Kushite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taharqa">Taharqa</a>. Assyrian control was later reasserted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>. The conquest marked the greatest territorial extent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/assyrian-conquest-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Assyrian conquest of Egypt" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nigyou · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Neo-Assyrian_map_824-671_BC.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
